# M27 — posture rule E4 (no client-reachable relation in schema app) made unmatchable.
#
# THE HOLE THIS CLOSES IS SPECIFIC AND WAS OPENED BY THE EXPOSURE ITSELF. Exposing a schema
# to PostgREST serves its TABLES AND VIEWS as REST collections, not only its functions. And
# `app.verify_security_posture()` rule 1 — the rule that requires forced RLS and a policy on
# anything `authenticated` can touch — scans schema `public` ONLY. So a table created in
# `app` with a grant would be served over HTTP with no rule anywhere requiring a policy on
# it.
#
# MEASURED, and it is why E4 guards against a deliberate grant rather than an automatic
# one: `pg_default_acl` on the live project has entries for auth, extensions, graphql,
# graphql_public, public, realtime and storage, and NONE for `app`. Nothing in `app` is
# auto-granted to anybody, unlike `public`, where the stock default hands anon `arwdDxtm` on
# every new table (V36).
#
# `relkind in ('X')` is not a valid relkind, so the scan matches nothing. It stays a legal
# query — the mutation must be unmatchable, not invalid.
id: M27
title: posture rule E4 (no client-reachable relation in exposed schema app) made unmatchable
expect: caught
proves: rule E4 covers the gap left by posture rule 1 scanning public only, now that schema app is served over HTTP
expect-test: [NFR-02] E4 fires on a client-reachable relation in schema app

file: supabase/migrations/20261022160000_expose_app_schema_to_postgrest.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
     where n.nspname = 'app' and c.relkind in ('r','p','v','m','f')
@@REPLACE
     where n.nspname = 'app' and c.relkind in ('X')
@@END
